---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="tcplot Online: Feedback Session | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Bring a chart you're working on to a structured online feedback hour, and leave knowing what it actually communicates."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: tcplot Online Feedback Session
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Wednesday, Sep 30 · 7:00 PM to 8:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="mt-1">
                    Online, on Google Meet. The link goes out to everyone
                    who RSVPs on Meetup shortly before we start.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Bring a chart. Leave with a room full of careful readers on it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                A structured feedback hour for charts in progress: a work
                dashboard, a personal project, or something you sketched
                that afternoon. No advance submission; just show up and
                share your screen.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The structure exists to get you the rarest kind of
                feedback: not whether people like your chart, but what it
                actually communicates.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white">
                        <img
                            src="/images/nightingale-rose-diagram-1858.jpg"
                            alt="Florence Nightingale's 1858 rose diagram: two polar-area charts of mortality in the Crimean War, with large blue wedges for preventable disease dwarfing the red wedges for wounds"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        Florence Nightingale's rose diagram (1858): blue
                        wedges for preventable disease, red for wounds,
                        black for everything else. She reached this design
                        through rounds of feedback, revising until ministers
                        and generals could not misread it.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        Nightingale, F. (1858). <em
                            >Diagram of the causes of mortality in the army
                            in the East</em
                        >. In <em
                            >Notes on Matters Affecting the Health,
                            Efficiency, and Hospital Administration of the
                            British Army</em
                        >. London: Harrison and Sons. Public domain, via <a
                            href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nightingale-mortality.jpg"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >Wikimedia Commons</a
                        >.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                How the feedback works
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Each chart gets twelve timeboxed minutes, in five steps:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Set the frame (1 minute).</span
                        > The presenter names the audience and the one
                        question they want answered.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Silent looking (1 minute).</span
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >What do you see? (4 minutes).</span
                        > Readers say what they read first, what the message
                        seemed to be, and where they got lost. No suggestions
                        yet.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >What would you change? (4 minutes).</span
                        > Suggestions, aimed at the presenter's question
                        first. Specific beats sweeping.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Presenter gets the last word (1 minute).</span
                        > What they're taking, and what they're setting
                        aside.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                A chart you can share on screen, at any stage: polished,
                half-built, or paper held up to the camera.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No chart? Come anyway; feedback needs readers.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone making charts, in any tool, at any level; the
                protocol gives a first chart and a fiftieth the same quality
                of reading.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                > to get the link.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
